          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "Iron is needed to make hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. When iron stores run low, the body makes fewer hemoglobin molecules, and the red cells it does make are smaller and paler than normal (*Iron-deficiency anemia*, General clinical reference). With less oxygen reaching the tissues, a person may feel tired.\n\nI could not find passages in my sources that explain the role of iron in muscle, the other links between iron deficiency and fatigue, or how strongly these factors contribute in any one person. I can't fill that gap from general knowledge, so please ask a clinician or check a more detailed source for those points.\n\nTwo other points from the sources:\n\n- Iron deficiency can affect symptoms independently. Heart-failure guidance says iron deficiency is looked for and corrected because it is common and independently affects symptoms (*Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction*). This is about people with heart failure, not a general rule.\n- In adults, low iron usually means the body is losing iron, most often through bleeding. The anemia reference says this is why it is treated as a finding that needs an explanation, not a diagnosis on its own.\n\nIf you feel tired and think you might have low iron, a clinician can check your blood count and iron levels and look for the cause. Seek urgent care straight away if you notice black or tarry stools, blood in your stool or vomit, heavy bleeding, chest pain, or severe breathlessness, or if you feel faint.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
